              answer:
                "- In 2025, global average temperatures rose about 1.3°C above pre-industrial levels, making it the third warmest year on record, with some reports indicating around 1.43°C above 1850-1900 averages[1][3][5][6][17].  \n- Arctic sea ice extent reached record low levels in several months of 2025, including the lowest annual average or second lowest on record, with September and other months showing historic lows[3][10][16][19].  \n- Renewable energy's share of global electricity increased, with renewables providing around 35% of global electricity by 2025, up from about 29% in 2023, with wind and solar leading the growth[1][15].  \n- Carbon emissions from fossil fuels showed mixed trends: some sources report a slight decrease in power sector emissions by about 10% year-on-year due to renewables, but overall fossil fuel CO2 emissions increased by about 1.1% in 2025, reaching record levels around 38.1 billion tons CO2, with atmospheric CO2 concentrations reaching approximately 425-426 ppm[2][4][9][14].  \n- Despite some reductions in fossil fuel share in electricity generation, total atmospheric CO2 continued to rise, reaching levels not seen in millions of years, driven by ongoing emissions and reduced natural carbon sink effectiveness[1][7][8][19].",
