                  title: "Climate and Energy Summary - World Energy Data",
                  snippet:
                    "Updated November 2025. The statistics below depend on different datasets, for which latest years differ. Global Warming The global mean near-surface temperature in 2024 was 1.55°C above the pre-industrial 1850–1900 average.1 The past ten years 2015-2024 are the ten warmest years on record.1 Long-term global warming, as of 2024, is about 1.3°C compared to the…\nUpdated November 2025. The statistics below depend on different datasets, for which latest years differ. Global Warming The global mean near-surface temperature in 2024 was 1.55°C above the pre-industrial 1850–1900 average.1 The past ten years 2015-2024 are the ten warmest years on record.1 Long-term global warming, as of 2024, is about 1.3°C compared to the…\nFossil fuel CO2 emissions reached a record 35.49 billion tonnes in 2024.14 · Coal, oil and gas production all reached record highs in 2024.15 · Global investment in fossil fuels increased by 2.4% in 2024, reaching $1.116 trillion.16, 17 · Surplus global oil supply capacity will reach unprecedented levels by 2030.18 · Vitol, one of the most profitable companies in the world on a per employee basis, stated in 2025 that “Global demand for oil will not fall until at least 2040.”19\nThe global average annual atmospheric CO2 concentration reached a record of 422.8ppm (parts per million) in 2024, increasing at a record rate of 3.7ppm/year.4, 5 This level is unprecedented in 14 million years,6 and the rate is unprecedented in the past 66 million years.7 · Today’s rate of atmospheric carbon dioxide increase is 10 times faster than at any other point in the past 50,000 years.8\nThe most optimistic scenario, which combines the full implementation of conditional NDCs and all net-zero pledges – which now excludes the US – would limit warming this century to 1.9°C. To return to 1.5°C by 2100, 260 to 610 billions of tonnes of CO2 will need to be removed from the atmosphere and stored permanently.3",
